{
  "concept": "The same two events — light pulse emitted and received — trace a vertical path of length 2D in the astronaut's frame but a longer diagonal path 2s in Earth's frame, and since light's speed is fixed, the Earth observer must measure a longer elapsed time Δt than the astronaut's Δτ.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "space shuttle side view at top with magnifying lines to inset (context only)",
    "panel (a): rounded rectangular cabin frame containing orange-suited astronaut labeled 'Astronaut', analog clock labeled Δτ, yellow-burst 'Light source', 'Receiver', 'Mirror' at top, teal up-and-down pulse arrows, vertical bracket labeled D",
    "panel (b): three dashed-outline cabin snapshots in a row labeled 'Beginning event', mirror-reflection midpoint, 'Ending event'; purple diagonal light path with both segments labeled s; horizontal brackets below labeled L = vΔt/2 and L; standing figure on tan ground labeled 'Observer on Earth' with clock labeled Δt",
    "panel (c): isosceles triangle with slanted sides labeled s, vertical altitude D, bases labeled L = vΔt/2 and L, formula s = √(D² + L²) above",
    "clock readouts Δτ (astronaut) and Δt (Earth observer) shown side by side for comparison"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "observerFrame",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Reference frame",
      "options": ["Astronaut (shuttle)", "Observer on Earth"],
      "default": "Astronaut (shuttle)",
      "teaches": "The two observers watch the identical two events but see different light paths — straight up-and-down 2D in the shuttle frame, two diagonals 2s in the Earth frame — so path length, and therefore elapsed time, is frame-dependent"
    },
    {
      "id": "shuttleSpeed",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Shuttle speed v",
      "range": [0, 0.9, 0.05],
      "default": 0.5,
      "teaches": "Increasing v stretches the horizontal shift L, lengthens the diagonal path s = √(D² + L²), and widens the gap between Δt and Δτ — time dilation has a purely geometric origin; at v = 0 the two frames agree"
    },
    {
      "id": "eventStep",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Step the pulse",
      "states": ["idle", "emission", "reflection", "reception"],
      "default": "idle",
      "teaches": "Emission, mirror reflection, and reception are the same three events in both frames — the observers disagree about the path length and time between them, never about what happened"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The astronaut's clock",
      "narration": "In the shuttle frame the pulse travels straight up to the mirror and back, covering exactly 2D, and the astronaut's clock reads Δτ. The apparatus does not move relative to her.",
      "state": { "observerFrame": "Astronaut (shuttle)", "eventStep": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Same events, seen from Earth",
      "narration": "Switch to the Earth frame: between emission and reception the cabin has moved, so the same pulse must chase the mirror along two diagonals totaling 2s > 2D. Because light's speed is the same for both observers, the longer path means a longer measured time Δt.",
      "state": { "observerFrame": "Observer on Earth", "eventStep": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Speed sets the dilation",
      "narration": "Drag the speed slider: faster shuttle motion spreads the snapshots apart, grows L and s, and the Earth clock Δt pulls further ahead of Δτ. At v = 0 the diagonal collapses to the vertical and the clocks agree.",
      "state": { "observerFrame": "Observer on Earth", "shuttleSpeed": 0.8, "eventStep": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Step through the shared events",
      "narration": "Step the pulse through emission, reflection, and reception: the same event markers light up in the cabin snapshots and in the triangle of panel (c). Both observers agree on the sequence of events — only the geometry between them differs.",
      "state": { "observerFrame": "Observer on Earth", "eventStep": "reception" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Because the speed of light is the same in every frame, the longer diagonal path seen from Earth must correspond to a longer elapsed time — Δt > Δτ follows from s = √(D² + L²) alone, with no new physics.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Three linked sub-panels shown together: (a) astronaut view on the left — rounded cabin frame, teal vertical double-arrow pulse, bracket D, clock Δτ; (b) Earth view center — three dashed cabin snapshots left to right, purple zigzag pulse path with segments labeled s, brackets L = vΔt/2 and L below, Earth observer figure with clock Δt; (c) geometric reduction below — isosceles triangle, altitude D, bases L, sides s, formula s = √(D² + L²) above. Toggle emphasizes one apparatus view at a time (panel (a) style vs panel (b) style) while panel (c)'s triangle is always visible as the bridge. The slider spreads/closes the three snapshots and stretches the triangle's base; clock readouts update numerically (Δτ fixed, Δt = γΔτ). The event-step button highlights the active event location simultaneously in the cabin snapshots and on the triangle vertex/foot. Colors: teal pulse in astronaut frame, purple in Earth frame, yellow burst for source, gray dashed snapshot outlines, tan ground plane."
}

